1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,799
"Love is measured in dollars, dear.

2
00:00:02,799 --> 00:00:04,239
Everyone knows that."

3
00:00:04,689 --> 00:00:11,769
My future mother-in-law, Diane Whitmore, slid a single sheet of paper across the white tablecloth.

4
00:00:11,769 --> 00:00:18,633
At the very top, in bold letters, it said: Bride's family contribution, fifty thousand dollars.

5
00:00:19,083 --> 00:00:21,576
My father was standing in front of her.

6
00:00:21,576 --> 00:00:23,686
Nobody had asked him to sit down.

7
00:00:23,686 --> 00:00:28,418
In his hands he held a set of walnut serving spoons he had carved himself.

8
00:00:28,418 --> 00:00:32,765
Three months of evenings, sanding by the light of his workshop lamp.

9
00:00:33,215 --> 00:00:35,370
And at that moment, I smiled to myself.

10
00:00:35,370 --> 00:00:42,054
Because I already knew that before the night was over, that proud woman would be lowering her head in front of my father.

11
00:00:42,504 --> 00:00:43,481
How did I know?

12
00:00:43,481 --> 00:00:46,867
Because I was living that night for the second time.

13
00:00:47,767 --> 00:00:54,814
Hello, friends, and welcome to Life Story, where the hard moments of life finally get the ending they deserve.

14
00:00:55,264 --> 00:01:02,613
Today's story is about a small-town carpenter who never had much money, but never once did anything to be ashamed of.

15
00:01:02,613 --> 00:01:07,260
And it's about his daughter, who was given a second chance to protect him.

16
00:01:07,710 --> 00:01:09,911
So pour yourself a cup of coffee.

17
00:01:09,911 --> 00:01:15,046
Listen while you drive, while you fold the laundry, while you rest your feet.

18
00:01:15,046 --> 00:01:16,847
Stay with me until the end.

19
00:01:16,847 --> 00:01:18,914
I promise, it will be worth it.

20
00:01:19,814 --> 00:01:21,769
My name is Grace Miller.

21
00:01:21,769 --> 00:01:29,345
I'm thirty-four years old, and I run a little bakery on Maple Street in our hometown in Ohio.

22
00:01:29,345 --> 00:01:31,789
It's called Grace's Bread Box.

23
00:01:31,789 --> 00:01:36,595
My father carved the sign himself, out of an old oak board.

24
00:01:37,045 --> 00:01:40,965
In my first life, I did what I was raised to do.

25
00:01:40,965 --> 00:01:42,354
I kept the peace.

26
00:01:42,354 --> 00:01:49,378
When my future mother-in-law humiliated my father, I lowered my eyes and said nothing.

27
00:01:49,378 --> 00:01:55,095
I told myself that once we were married, everything would settle down.

28
00:01:55,545 --> 00:01:58,963
One year after the wedding, I had lost everything.

29
00:01:59,413 --> 00:02:02,195
Diane handed me some papers one afternoon.

30
00:02:02,195 --> 00:02:04,646
"Just a family formality, sweetheart.

31
00:02:04,646 --> 00:02:05,971
Sign here and here."

32
00:02:05,971 --> 00:02:08,423
I signed them without reading a word.

33
00:02:08,423 --> 00:02:11,470
Those papers put a lien on my bakery building.

34
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:21,672
They made my shop the collateral for a two hundred thousand dollar loan Diane had taken out in secret, from a man who called himself an investment broker.

35
00:02:22,122 --> 00:02:28,997
The day the collectors came to my door, my father collapsed on the sidewalk in front of my bakery.

36
00:02:28,997 --> 00:02:30,541
He never got up again.

37
00:02:30,991 --> 00:02:43,406
At his funeral, Diane leaned over to a friend and said, loud enough for me to hear, "Well, this is what happens when families from different worlds try to mix."

38
00:02:44,306 --> 00:02:50,146
That night I fell asleep on my father's couch, holding the wooden spoons he had made for me.

39
00:02:50,596 --> 00:02:55,522
When I opened my eyes, morning sunlight was pouring through the kitchen window.

40
00:02:55,522 --> 00:02:57,579
My phone was on the coffee table.

41
00:02:57,579 --> 00:02:59,948
I picked it up and stared at the date.

42
00:02:59,948 --> 00:03:01,320
It was a year earlier.

43
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:04,063
It was the morning of the engagement dinner.

44
00:03:04,513 --> 00:03:06,324
I thought I was dreaming.

45
00:03:06,324 --> 00:03:07,484
Then I heard it.

46
00:03:07,484 --> 00:03:09,295
A cough from the kitchen.

47
00:03:09,295 --> 00:03:12,773
And the soft, steady sound of sandpaper on wood.

48
00:03:13,223 --> 00:03:14,469
I ran in, barefoot.

49
00:03:14,469 --> 00:03:18,799
My father turned around, holding a finished spoon up to the light.

50
00:03:19,249 --> 00:03:21,176
"Whoa, slow down, kiddo.

51
00:03:21,176 --> 00:03:23,826
You'll slip and break your neck."

52
00:03:24,276 --> 00:03:27,965
I threw my arms around him and cried like a little girl.

53
00:03:27,965 --> 00:03:30,072
He didn't understand, of course.

54
00:03:30,072 --> 00:03:33,695
He just patted my back the way he did when I was seven.

55
00:03:34,145 --> 00:03:34,427
"Ah.

56
00:03:34,427 --> 00:03:38,035
Getting cold feet about leaving your old man, huh?"

57
00:03:38,485 --> 00:03:40,849
Right then, I made a promise.

58
00:03:40,849 --> 00:03:43,458
This time, I would not lose him.

59
00:03:43,458 --> 00:03:44,600
Not like that.

60
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:45,904
Never like that.

61
00:03:46,804 --> 00:03:50,030
Let me tell you a little more about my father.

62
00:03:50,030 --> 00:03:50,802
Tom Miller.

63
00:03:51,252 --> 00:03:53,474
My mother passed away when I was seven.

64
00:03:53,474 --> 00:03:55,981
From that day on, it was just the two of us.

65
00:03:55,981 --> 00:03:58,830
Dad got up at five every morning to pack my lunch.

66
00:03:58,830 --> 00:04:07,035
In the evenings he set up a little desk for me in the corner of his workshop, and I did my homework surrounded by the smell of sawdust and pine.

67
00:04:07,485 --> 00:04:09,432
Dad was not a man of many words.

68
00:04:09,432 --> 00:04:10,952
He talked with his hands.

69
00:04:10,952 --> 00:04:13,264
Every birthday he carved me something.

70
00:04:13,264 --> 00:04:15,697
A little wooden dog when I turned seven.

71
00:04:15,697 --> 00:04:17,582
A pencil box when I turned ten.

72
00:04:17,582 --> 00:04:20,806
A bookshelf with my name on it when I turned fifteen.

73
00:04:20,806 --> 00:04:22,691
I still have every one of them.

74
00:04:23,141 --> 00:04:27,121
On the day I graduated from high school, Dad bought his first suit.

75
00:04:27,121 --> 00:04:30,269
He took my hand and said, "Gracie, I don't have much.

76
00:04:30,269 --> 00:04:34,724
But I've never done one thing in my life that would make you ashamed of me.

77
00:04:34,724 --> 00:04:36,506
That much, I can promise you."

78
00:04:36,956 --> 00:04:40,217
That was the same suit he wore to the engagement dinner.

79
00:04:40,667 --> 00:04:44,589
In my first life, I was embarrassed that the sleeves were too short.

80
00:04:44,589 --> 00:04:48,454
That is the one thing I have never been able to forgive myself for.

81
00:04:49,354 --> 00:04:52,193
That morning, I did three things.

82
00:04:52,643 --> 00:04:56,473
First, I practiced using the voice recorder on my phone.

83
00:04:56,473 --> 00:05:02,220
I checked again and again that it would pick up a conversation from inside my purse.

84
00:05:02,670 --> 00:05:06,856
Second, I wrote down everything I remembered from my first life.

85
00:05:06,856 --> 00:05:10,520
The lender was a company called Summit Capital Partners.

86
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:16,539
The broker was a man named Gary Pruitt, who wore a gold watch and called everybody "friend."

87
00:05:16,539 --> 00:05:21,772
And the day Diane would ask me to sign was exactly two weeks before the wedding.

88
00:05:22,222 --> 00:05:24,737
Third, I thought about one person.

89
00:05:24,737 --> 00:05:27,843
My future father-in-law, Richard Whitmore.

90
00:05:28,293 --> 00:05:31,396
In my first life, Richard hardly ever spoke.

91
00:05:31,396 --> 00:05:36,614
At family dinners he read the newspaper and nodded at whatever Diane said.

92
00:05:36,614 --> 00:05:38,870
I thought he simply didn't care.

93
00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:44,156
But at my father's funeral, Richard was the last to arrive and the last to leave.

94
00:05:44,156 --> 00:05:47,499
He stood in front of Dad's casket for a long, long time.

95
00:05:47,499 --> 00:05:52,156
And on his way out, he said something to me so quietly that no one else heard.

96
00:05:52,606 --> 00:05:54,569
"I found out too late.

97
00:05:54,569 --> 00:05:55,551
I'm sorry."

98
00:05:56,001 --> 00:05:59,049
Back then, I didn't understand what he meant.

99
00:05:59,049 --> 00:06:01,420
This time, I was going to find out.

100
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:07,159
My fiancé, Ethan Whitmore, was one of my regular customers.

101
00:06:07,609 --> 00:06:13,602
Three winters ago, he came in every morning at eight o'clock and bought two cinnamon rolls.

102
00:06:13,602 --> 00:06:18,147
Then came the big snowstorm, the day not a single customer showed up.

103
00:06:18,147 --> 00:06:24,206
Ethan walked in, shook the snow off his coat, and said, "I'll take everything you have left.

104
00:06:24,206 --> 00:06:26,446
I'll give them out at the office."

105
00:06:26,446 --> 00:06:28,817
That was the day we started talking.

106
00:06:29,267 --> 00:06:30,650
Ethan was a kind man.

107
00:06:30,650 --> 00:06:36,578
He used to stop by Dad's workshop on Friday nights and drink a beer with him on the porch.

108
00:06:36,578 --> 00:06:37,895
Dad liked him a lot.

109
00:06:38,345 --> 00:06:40,220
But Ethan had one weakness.

110
00:06:40,220 --> 00:06:43,554
In front of his mother, he could not say a word.

111
00:06:44,004 --> 00:06:47,926
In my first life, I believed love would be enough to fix that.

112
00:06:47,926 --> 00:06:48,558
It wasn't.

113
00:06:48,558 --> 00:06:52,101
Every time he stayed silent, I was standing there alone.

114
00:06:52,551 --> 00:06:55,599
This time, I couldn't change him by force.

115
00:06:55,599 --> 00:06:57,703
He had to choose for himself.

116
00:06:58,603 --> 00:07:01,562
The dinner was at the Ridgewood Country Club.

117
00:07:01,562 --> 00:07:03,601
Diane had chosen it, of course.

118
00:07:04,051 --> 00:07:08,501
That morning, my father took his one good suit out of the closet.

119
00:07:08,501 --> 00:07:11,103
The sleeves were still a little short.

120
00:07:11,103 --> 00:07:15,417
He stood in front of the mirror and retied his tie three times.

121
00:07:15,867 --> 00:07:17,091
"How do I look, Gracie?

122
00:07:17,091 --> 00:07:20,019
I don't want to embarrass you in front of those folks."

123
00:07:20,469 --> 00:07:24,519
"Dad, you look like the most handsome man in the world."

124
00:07:24,969 --> 00:07:27,230
And I meant every word.

125
00:07:28,130 --> 00:07:31,548
The insults started before we even walked in the door.

126
00:07:31,998 --> 00:07:37,133
Dad drove us there in his work truck, a twenty-year-old Ford with a ladder rack on top.

127
00:07:37,133 --> 00:07:41,677
He had taken it to the car wash that morning and polished it until it shined.

128
00:07:42,127 --> 00:07:46,411
Diane was getting out of her silver Mercedes when she saw the truck.

129
00:07:46,411 --> 00:07:47,861
She stopped and stared.

130
00:07:48,311 --> 00:07:48,804
"Oh, my.

131
00:07:48,804 --> 00:07:49,483
Tom, is it?

132
00:07:49,483 --> 00:07:52,199
Would you mind parking that around the back?

133
00:07:52,199 --> 00:07:53,680
By the dumpsters, maybe.

134
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:55,284
The valet will understand.

135
00:07:55,284 --> 00:07:58,617
It just doesn't look right next to the members' cars."

136
00:07:59,067 --> 00:08:02,053
Dad said, "Oh, sure, sure, no problem,"

137
00:08:02,053 --> 00:08:07,413
and moved the truck to the far end of the lot, next to the trash bins.

138
00:08:07,413 --> 00:08:12,696
Then he walked all the way back, wiping his forehead with his sleeve.

139
00:08:13,146 --> 00:08:19,072
That's when I noticed someone standing quietly by the entrance, watching the whole thing.

140
00:08:19,072 --> 00:08:20,071
It was Richard.

141
00:08:20,071 --> 00:08:21,869
He had come by taxi, alone.

142
00:08:21,869 --> 00:08:25,132
He looked at my father's truck for a long moment.

143
00:08:25,132 --> 00:08:28,195
Then he turned and went inside without a word.

144
00:08:29,095 --> 00:08:38,696
Inside the private dining room, Diane was seated at the head of the table, pearls at her throat, her designer handbag on the chair beside her.

145
00:08:38,696 --> 00:08:41,468
Ethan sat next to her, smiling nervously.

146
00:08:41,468 --> 00:08:45,660
His sister, Brittany, was taking pictures of the bread basket.

147
00:08:45,660 --> 00:08:49,514
Richard sat at the far end by the window, saying nothing.

148
00:08:49,964 --> 00:08:52,540
My father bowed his head politely.

149
00:08:52,540 --> 00:08:54,132
"Good evening, ma'am.

150
00:08:54,132 --> 00:08:56,329
I'm Tom Miller, Grace's dad."

151
00:08:56,779 --> 00:08:58,643
Diane looked him up and down.

152
00:08:58,643 --> 00:09:00,829
And she did not invite him to sit.

153
00:09:00,829 --> 00:09:03,851
There was an empty chair right in front of him.

154
00:09:03,851 --> 00:09:05,458
She just didn't offer it.

155
00:09:05,908 --> 00:09:08,915
Dad hesitated, then stayed on his feet.

156
00:09:08,915 --> 00:09:11,537
Inside my purse, I pressed record.

157
00:09:11,987 --> 00:09:16,668
Diane pulled an envelope out of her bag and unfolded a typed list.

158
00:09:17,118 --> 00:09:20,782
Bride's family contribution, fifty thousand dollars.

159
00:09:20,782 --> 00:09:23,107
Rehearsal dinner, eight thousand.

160
00:09:23,107 --> 00:09:24,657
Flowers, six thousand.

161
00:09:24,657 --> 00:09:26,277
A new suit for Richard.

162
00:09:26,277 --> 00:09:27,757
A fur coat for Diane.

163
00:09:27,757 --> 00:09:29,730
A designer bag for Brittany.

164
00:09:29,730 --> 00:09:33,323
Next to every item, there was nothing but a number.

165
00:09:33,773 --> 00:09:37,025
"These days we like to put everything in writing,"

166
00:09:37,025 --> 00:09:37,741
Diane said.

167
00:09:37,741 --> 00:09:40,083
"So there are no misunderstandings."

168
00:09:40,533 --> 00:09:42,287
My father took the paper.

169
00:09:42,287 --> 00:09:44,391
His hand was shaking a little.

170
00:09:44,391 --> 00:09:50,004
Added together, those numbers came to more than he had saved in his entire life.

171
00:09:50,454 --> 00:09:52,406
"Ma'am, I'll be honest with you.

172
00:09:52,406 --> 00:09:54,419
We don't have that kind of money.

173
00:09:54,419 --> 00:09:56,494
But I made something for the kids.

174
00:09:56,494 --> 00:09:58,080
Something from the heart."

175
00:09:58,530 --> 00:10:00,516
He unwrapped a soft cloth.

176
00:10:00,516 --> 00:10:06,398
Inside was a set of walnut serving spoons, the handles carved with two names.

177
00:10:06,398 --> 00:10:06,857
Ethan.

178
00:10:06,857 --> 00:10:07,315
Grace.

179
00:10:07,765 --> 00:10:09,395
The room went quiet.

180
00:10:09,845 --> 00:10:11,667
And then Diane laughed.

181
00:10:11,667 --> 00:10:14,044
Out loud, and for a long time.

182
00:10:14,944 --> 00:10:16,282
"Wooden spoons?

183
00:10:16,282 --> 00:10:16,996
Oh, Tom.

184
00:10:16,996 --> 00:10:18,513
That's adorable."

185
00:10:18,963 --> 00:10:23,592
She picked one up between two fingers, as if it were something dirty.

186
00:10:24,042 --> 00:10:26,521
"Do you know what kind of family we are?

187
00:10:26,521 --> 00:10:30,735
Richard owns one of the biggest construction companies in the state.

188
00:10:30,735 --> 00:10:35,878
When we have guests, we can't exactly serve them with something from a craft fair."

189
00:10:36,328 --> 00:10:39,815
"I just wanted to give them something with love in it,"

190
00:10:39,815 --> 00:10:41,273
my father said quietly.

191
00:10:41,723 --> 00:10:43,299
Diane cut him off.

192
00:10:43,749 --> 00:10:46,148
"Love is measured in dollars, dear.

193
00:10:46,148 --> 00:10:47,588
Everyone knows that."

194
00:10:48,038 --> 00:10:51,156
She set the spoon down on the edge of the table.

195
00:10:51,156 --> 00:10:53,950
It rolled off and clattered onto the floor.

196
00:10:53,950 --> 00:10:56,159
My father bent down to pick it up.

197
00:10:56,159 --> 00:10:59,083
The knee of his only suit touched the carpet.

198
00:10:59,533 --> 00:11:00,594
"Let me be frank.

199
00:11:00,594 --> 00:11:05,527
If you can't manage even this much, maybe this marriage isn't such a good idea.

200
00:11:05,527 --> 00:11:07,276
Ethan has plenty of options.

201
00:11:07,276 --> 00:11:10,210
Very good families have been asking about him."

202
00:11:10,660 --> 00:11:11,861
I looked at Ethan.

203
00:11:11,861 --> 00:11:14,130
He was staring at his water glass.

204
00:11:14,130 --> 00:11:15,932
Just like in my first life.

205
00:11:15,932 --> 00:11:18,868
At this exact moment, he never said a thing.

206
00:11:19,318 --> 00:11:20,084
And Richard.

207
00:11:20,084 --> 00:11:22,317
Richard was looking out the window.

208
00:11:22,317 --> 00:11:25,572
But this time, I saw something I had missed before.

209
00:11:25,572 --> 00:11:31,315
Under the table, his hand was clenched into a fist so tight his knuckles had turned white.

210
00:11:32,215 --> 00:11:33,739
It didn't stop there.

211
00:11:34,189 --> 00:11:39,052
Diane called the waiter over and ordered the most expensive things on the menu.

212
00:11:39,052 --> 00:11:40,098
The filet mignon.

213
00:11:40,098 --> 00:11:41,144
The lobster tail.

214
00:11:41,144 --> 00:11:44,406
A bottle of wine that cost more than my weekly sales.

215
00:11:44,406 --> 00:11:47,237
Then she glanced at my father, still standing.

216
00:11:47,687 --> 00:11:49,473
"And what will you have, Tom?

217
00:11:49,473 --> 00:11:52,306
Oh, I suppose it's awkward to eat standing up.

218
00:11:52,306 --> 00:11:53,784
Maybe just some coffee."

219
00:11:54,234 --> 00:11:57,994
So my father drank a glass of water for the entire evening.

220
00:11:57,994 --> 00:12:00,416
Brittany posted photos of her lobster.

221
00:12:00,416 --> 00:12:02,073
Ethan stared at his plate.

222
00:12:02,523 --> 00:12:05,994
When the check came, Diane slid it toward my father.

223
00:12:06,444 --> 00:12:10,212
"Nowadays both families split these dinners, don't they?

224
00:12:10,212 --> 00:12:13,913
It's nine hundred dollars, so your half is four fifty."

225
00:12:14,363 --> 00:12:16,463
My father took out his debit card.

226
00:12:16,463 --> 00:12:21,466
The money in that account came from three rocking chairs he had built that month.

227
00:12:21,466 --> 00:12:25,728
He paid for a dinner he never ate, and he thanked them for having us.

228
00:12:26,628 --> 00:12:29,949
Honestly, I wanted to flip that table over.

229
00:12:29,949 --> 00:12:31,572
My blood was boiling.

230
00:12:32,022 --> 00:12:33,055
But I held it in.

231
00:12:33,055 --> 00:12:40,288
If I lost my temper now, Diane would simply call me rude and ungrateful, and Ethan would be told to find a better girl.

232
00:12:40,288 --> 00:12:43,874
And those loan papers would find another way into my hands.

233
00:12:43,874 --> 00:12:45,211
I didn't need a scene.

234
00:12:45,211 --> 00:12:46,123
I needed proof.

235
00:12:46,123 --> 00:12:48,493
Proof she couldn't talk her way out of.

236
00:12:48,943 --> 00:12:49,251
"Mrs.

237
00:12:49,251 --> 00:12:51,715
Whitmore, thank you for being so clear,"

238
00:12:51,715 --> 00:12:52,700
I said politely.

239
00:12:52,700 --> 00:12:56,149
"My father and I will talk it over and get back to you."

240
00:12:56,599 --> 00:12:59,808
Diane smiled like a woman who had just won.

241
00:13:00,258 --> 00:13:03,283
On the drive home, my father didn't say one word.

242
00:13:03,283 --> 00:13:08,159
He kept the cloth with the spoons on his lap and watched the dark fields go by.

243
00:13:08,159 --> 00:13:11,308
When we pulled into the driveway, he finally spoke.

244
00:13:11,758 --> 00:13:14,360
"Gracie, I'm sorry your dad isn't more.

245
00:13:14,360 --> 00:13:18,965
Watching them look down on you because of me, that's the worst part."

246
00:13:19,415 --> 00:13:21,258
"Dad, don't you ever say that.

247
00:13:21,258 --> 00:13:23,777
You are not the one who should be sorry."

248
00:13:24,227 --> 00:13:28,322
That night the light in his workshop stayed on until two in the morning.

249
00:13:28,322 --> 00:13:32,076
When I peeked through the door, he was sanding those spoons again.

250
00:13:32,076 --> 00:13:34,407
Spoons that were already smooth as glass.

251
00:13:34,407 --> 00:13:36,853
That was how my father carried his sadness.

252
00:13:37,753 --> 00:13:41,732
The next morning, Diane called my father directly.

253
00:13:41,732 --> 00:13:44,119
She didn't even go through me.

254
00:13:44,569 --> 00:13:48,303
He had her on speaker, and her voice was ice-cold.

255
00:13:48,753 --> 00:13:50,851
"Tom, have you thought about it?

256
00:13:50,851 --> 00:13:53,867
We've booked the venue, we can't wait forever.

257
00:13:53,867 --> 00:13:56,293
And frankly, our relatives will talk.

258
00:13:56,293 --> 00:14:01,801
If the bride's side shows up with wooden spoons, what am I supposed to tell people?"

259
00:14:02,251 --> 00:14:05,668
"Ma'am, if you could just give me a little time."

260
00:14:06,118 --> 00:14:08,283
"I've given you plenty of time.

261
00:14:08,283 --> 00:14:09,610
And one more thing.

262
00:14:09,610 --> 00:14:15,966
At the wedding, if you're sitting there all by yourself, it looks a little sad, doesn't it?

263
00:14:15,966 --> 00:14:18,131
Can't you bring some relatives?

264
00:14:18,131 --> 00:14:21,902
The guest numbers on your side are just embarrassing."

265
00:14:22,352 --> 00:14:25,285
My father said, "Yes, ma'am, I understand,"

266
00:14:25,285 --> 00:14:26,103
and hung up.

267
00:14:26,103 --> 00:14:31,560
That afternoon, in the pouring rain, without an umbrella, he walked to the bank.

268
00:14:32,010 --> 00:14:35,985
The balance in his savings account was twelve thousand dollars.

269
00:14:35,985 --> 00:14:38,950
Everything he had saved in forty years of work.

270
00:14:39,850 --> 00:14:43,900
That weekend, strange rumors started going around town.

271
00:14:44,350 --> 00:14:45,275
"Did you hear?

272
00:14:45,275 --> 00:14:49,768
The Miller girl from the bakery is marrying into the Whitmore money.

273
00:14:49,768 --> 00:14:50,495
Smart girl.

274
00:14:50,495 --> 00:14:52,874
She knows exactly what she's doing."

275
00:14:53,324 --> 00:14:55,493
The rumors came from Diane's friends.

276
00:14:55,493 --> 00:15:02,058
Diane brought three of them into my bakery, ordered lattes, and talked loudly enough for every customer to hear.

277
00:15:02,508 --> 00:15:04,564
"Girls these days are so clever.

278
00:15:04,564 --> 00:15:08,610
Walk in with nothing and walk out with a construction fortune."

279
00:15:09,060 --> 00:15:12,005
"In our day, we'd never have dreamed of it."

280
00:15:12,455 --> 00:15:12,712
Mrs.

281
00:15:12,712 --> 00:15:18,037
Patterson, an old regular of mine, reached across the counter and squeezed my hand.

282
00:15:18,037 --> 00:15:20,154
"Don't you listen to them, honey.

283
00:15:20,154 --> 00:15:22,400
This whole town knows who you are."

284
00:15:22,850 --> 00:15:26,157
But that evening, a few regulars didn't come back.

285
00:15:26,157 --> 00:15:29,993
After closing, I kneaded tomorrow's dough alone and cried.

286
00:15:29,993 --> 00:15:34,953
I kept my chin up the whole time, so my tears wouldn't fall into the bread.

287
00:15:35,853 --> 00:15:42,693
A week later, Diane came back to my bakery, alone this time, without calling ahead.

288
00:15:43,143 --> 00:15:45,460
She walked slowly around the shop.

289
00:15:45,460 --> 00:15:46,619
The display case.

290
00:15:46,619 --> 00:15:48,050
The sourdough loaves.

291
00:15:48,050 --> 00:15:50,980
The wooden trays my father had made for me.

292
00:15:51,430 --> 00:15:53,389
"It's smaller than I expected.

293
00:15:53,389 --> 00:15:55,022
But the location is good.

294
00:15:55,022 --> 00:15:57,373
I hear you own the building, Grace?"

295
00:15:57,823 --> 00:16:00,455
I had been waiting for that question.

296
00:16:00,455 --> 00:16:03,870
In my first life, she asked the very same thing.

297
00:16:04,320 --> 00:16:07,325
The building had been left to me by my grandmother.

298
00:16:07,325 --> 00:16:13,629
Before she passed, she told me, "Gracie, this building is what your father paid for with his own two hands.

299
00:16:13,629 --> 00:16:14,454
Keep it safe."

300
00:16:14,454 --> 00:16:20,581
I found out later that when Dad was young, he had worked himself to the bone to pay off Grandma's debts.

301
00:16:21,031 --> 00:16:21,858
"Yes, ma'am.

302
00:16:21,858 --> 00:16:23,923
My grandmother left it to me."

303
00:16:24,373 --> 00:16:25,076
"Wonderful.

304
00:16:25,076 --> 00:16:27,759
Once you're married, we're all one family.

305
00:16:27,759 --> 00:16:31,593
If the family ever needs a little help, you'll do your part.

306
00:16:31,593 --> 00:16:34,212
That's what a good daughter-in-law does."

307
00:16:34,662 --> 00:16:37,174
She left without buying a single thing.

308
00:16:37,174 --> 00:16:41,554
On her way out, she flicked my father's wooden tray with one finger.

309
00:16:42,004 --> 00:16:44,490
"And get rid of these country things.

310
00:16:44,490 --> 00:16:45,632
They look cheap."

311
00:16:46,532 --> 00:16:50,740
That Saturday, Ethan's sister Brittany came in with her friends.

312
00:16:51,190 --> 00:16:57,449
She took pictures of everything, then held up one of my father's wooden trays and giggled.

313
00:16:57,899 --> 00:17:02,719
"Oh my gosh, Grace, is this, like, a farmhouse theme?

314
00:17:02,719 --> 00:17:04,264
It's so grandpa."

315
00:17:04,714 --> 00:17:06,059
Her friends laughed.

316
00:17:06,059 --> 00:17:11,102
That night, a photo of my display case appeared on Brittany's social media.

317
00:17:11,102 --> 00:17:15,338
Under it, she had written, "My brother's fiancée's little shop.

318
00:17:15,338 --> 00:17:16,010
Cute vibe.

319
00:17:16,010 --> 00:17:19,237
But, like, a totally different world from ours."

320
00:17:19,687 --> 00:17:22,942
The comments filled up with laughing emojis.

321
00:17:22,942 --> 00:17:24,421
I took a screenshot.

322
00:17:24,421 --> 00:17:26,196
Not because I was angry.

323
00:17:26,196 --> 00:17:31,892
Because one day, I knew, I would need to show all of this, exactly as it was.

324
00:17:32,342 --> 00:17:34,666
The next morning, my father stopped by.

325
00:17:34,666 --> 00:17:39,492
He picked up one of the wooden trays and quietly started to slip it into his bag.

326
00:17:39,942 --> 00:17:41,993
"Dad, what are you doing?"

327
00:17:42,443 --> 00:17:45,786
"I heard people were making fun of you because of these.

328
00:17:45,786 --> 00:17:47,756
I'll buy you some nice new ones."

329
00:17:48,206 --> 00:17:49,520
"Dad, put that back.

330
00:17:49,520 --> 00:17:52,938
That tray is the most valuable thing in this store."

331
00:17:53,838 --> 00:17:56,783
From that day on, I moved quietly.

332
00:17:57,233 --> 00:18:03,779
First, I went to the office of Summit Capital Partners, pretending to ask about a small business loan.

333
00:18:03,779 --> 00:18:07,758
On the wall by the reception desk was a row of business cards.

334
00:18:07,758 --> 00:18:11,544
One of them said, "Gary Pruitt, Senior Investment Advisor."

335
00:18:11,994 --> 00:18:13,936
I took a picture of it.

336
00:18:14,386 --> 00:18:19,685
Next, I went to the county recorder's office and pulled the records for my building.

337
00:18:19,685 --> 00:18:21,262
Nothing had happened yet.

338
00:18:21,262 --> 00:18:22,524
The title was clean.

339
00:18:22,524 --> 00:18:26,940
That same day, I signed up for the county's free property fraud alert.

340
00:18:26,940 --> 00:18:31,987
If anyone filed anything against my property, I would get an email within hours.

341
00:18:31,987 --> 00:18:35,267
In my first life, I never knew that service existed.

342
00:18:35,717 --> 00:18:38,715
Finally, I met Ethan for coffee.

343
00:18:39,165 --> 00:18:42,005
"Ethan, is your mom having money trouble?"

344
00:18:42,455 --> 00:18:43,754
His face froze.

345
00:18:43,754 --> 00:18:46,265
He hesitated for a long time.

346
00:18:46,265 --> 00:18:48,083
Then it all came out.

347
00:18:48,533 --> 00:18:52,349
"She put money into some investment a friend recommended.

348
00:18:52,349 --> 00:18:53,152
It went bad.

349
00:18:53,152 --> 00:18:54,290
Dad doesn't know.

350
00:18:54,290 --> 00:18:56,633
She made me swear not to tell him."

351
00:18:57,083 --> 00:18:58,238
"How much?"

352
00:18:58,688 --> 00:19:00,325
"About two hundred thousand.

353
00:19:00,325 --> 00:19:02,488
But Grace, it's our family's problem.

354
00:19:02,488 --> 00:19:04,476
You don't need to worry about it."

355
00:19:04,926 --> 00:19:08,860
In my first life, at this exact moment, I said, "Okay."

356
00:19:08,860 --> 00:19:14,081
And that two hundred thousand dollars ended up being paid with my bakery.

357
00:19:14,531 --> 00:19:17,371
This time, I said something different.

358
00:19:17,821 --> 00:19:20,952
"Ethan, it's going to be my problem too.

359
00:19:20,952 --> 00:19:21,813
Very soon."

360
00:19:22,713 --> 00:19:26,465
Three days later, a stranger walked into the bakery.

361
00:19:26,465 --> 00:19:28,846
Black leather jacket, gold watch.

362
00:19:28,846 --> 00:19:29,712
Gary Pruitt.

363
00:19:30,162 --> 00:19:34,034
He picked up a muffin and looked around the shop for a long time.

364
00:19:34,034 --> 00:19:36,893
Like a man putting a price on everything he saw.

365
00:19:37,343 --> 00:19:38,118
"Nice place.

366
00:19:38,118 --> 00:19:39,280
Good foot traffic.

367
00:19:39,280 --> 00:19:40,571
You're Grace Miller?

368
00:19:40,571 --> 00:19:43,025
I hear you're about to become family."

369
00:19:43,475 --> 00:19:44,787
"And you are?"

370
00:19:45,237 --> 00:19:48,994
"Just a friend helping your future mother-in-law with some business.

371
00:19:48,994 --> 00:19:51,204
There'll be some paperwork for you soon.

372
00:19:51,204 --> 00:19:52,972
I'm sure you'll be cooperative."

373
00:19:53,422 --> 00:19:56,064
He left his business card on the counter.

374
00:19:56,064 --> 00:19:57,996
At the door, he turned around.

375
00:19:58,446 --> 00:20:00,976
"Families help each other, right, friend?

376
00:20:00,976 --> 00:20:03,444
When they don't, life gets complicated."

377
00:20:03,894 --> 00:20:05,369
That wasn't a request.

378
00:20:05,369 --> 00:20:06,441
It was a threat.

379
00:20:06,441 --> 00:20:09,994
And the phone in my apron pocket recorded every word.

380
00:20:10,894 --> 00:20:14,487
That Saturday, Diane showed up at my father's workshop.

381
00:20:14,487 --> 00:20:17,101
She brought Ethan and Brittany with her.

382
00:20:17,551 --> 00:20:23,812
The workshop sat at the end of our road, an old barn Dad had fixed up himself.

383
00:20:23,812 --> 00:20:26,381
It smelled of cedar and sawdust.

384
00:20:26,381 --> 00:20:28,228
It was where I grew up.

385
00:20:28,678 --> 00:20:31,831
Diane walked in holding a tissue over her nose.

386
00:20:32,281 --> 00:20:33,534
"Goodness, the dust.

387
00:20:33,534 --> 00:20:35,225
Tom, do you live out here?"

388
00:20:35,675 --> 00:20:37,786
"No, ma'am, this is where I work.

389
00:20:37,786 --> 00:20:39,513
Can I get you some coffee?"

390
00:20:39,963 --> 00:20:40,943
"No, thank you.

391
00:20:40,943 --> 00:20:42,118
We won't be long."

392
00:20:42,568 --> 00:20:45,593
She looked at a rocking chair on the workbench.

393
00:20:45,593 --> 00:20:48,811
Dad was building it for the senior center in town.

394
00:20:48,811 --> 00:20:49,776
Free of charge.

395
00:20:50,226 --> 00:20:52,475
"How much do you get for one of these?

396
00:20:52,475 --> 00:20:53,482
A couple hundred?

397
00:20:53,482 --> 00:20:56,855
At this rate, when exactly will you have fifty thousand?"

398
00:20:57,305 --> 00:20:58,597
Brittany snickered.

399
00:20:58,597 --> 00:21:01,724
Ethan stood by the door, looking at his phone.

400
00:21:02,174 --> 00:21:05,154
"If it's really that hard, there's an easy way.

401
00:21:05,154 --> 00:21:06,232
Grace's building.

402
00:21:06,232 --> 00:21:09,340
Take out a small loan against it and you're done.

403
00:21:09,340 --> 00:21:11,750
It'll be family property soon anyway."

404
00:21:12,200 --> 00:21:14,987
My father's face went white.

405
00:21:15,437 --> 00:21:16,229
"Ma'am, no.

406
00:21:16,229 --> 00:21:19,397
That building came from Grace's grandmother.

407
00:21:19,397 --> 00:21:20,908
It belongs to Grace."

408
00:21:21,358 --> 00:21:21,909
"Oh, Tom.

409
00:21:21,909 --> 00:21:27,107
You're going to marry off your daughter and keep everything locked up for yourselves?

410
00:21:27,107 --> 00:21:29,614
What does that say about how you see us?"

411
00:21:30,514 --> 00:21:34,196
Three weeks before the wedding, I went to choose my dress.

412
00:21:34,646 --> 00:21:37,866
Diane looked at the one I loved and shook her head.

413
00:21:37,866 --> 00:21:40,012
Then she turned to the saleswoman.

414
00:21:40,462 --> 00:21:41,360
"Not that one.

415
00:21:41,360 --> 00:21:43,669
Show us the most basic one you have.

416
00:21:43,669 --> 00:21:49,506
The bride's side isn't contributing anything, so there's no reason for us to go overboard."

417
00:21:49,956 --> 00:21:52,859
The saleswoman looked embarrassed for me.

418
00:21:52,859 --> 00:21:54,983
I smiled and said, "It's fine.

419
00:21:54,983 --> 00:21:56,895
The simple one is perfect."

420
00:21:57,345 --> 00:22:02,237
But what Diane said next made my whole body go cold.

421
00:22:02,687 --> 00:22:05,225
"And Grace, about walking down the aisle.

422
00:22:05,225 --> 00:22:09,496
If your father comes in that old suit, it's going to ruin the photos.

423
00:22:09,496 --> 00:22:12,405
Why doesn't Richard's brother walk you instead?

424
00:22:12,405 --> 00:22:14,262
Lots of families do that now."

425
00:22:14,712 --> 00:22:15,723
I couldn't answer.

426
00:22:15,723 --> 00:22:17,746
My father walking me down the aisle.

427
00:22:17,746 --> 00:22:20,498
He had talked about it since I was a little girl.

428
00:22:20,498 --> 00:22:22,970
"That's the one day I'm living for, Gracie."

429
00:22:23,420 --> 00:22:26,573
That night, I couldn't bring myself to tell him.

430
00:22:27,473 --> 00:22:31,207
A week later, the invitation proofs arrived.

431
00:22:31,657 --> 00:22:31,855
Mr.

432
00:22:31,855 --> 00:22:32,384
and Mrs.

433
00:22:32,384 --> 00:22:36,021
Richard Whitmore, printed large and elegant at the top.

434
00:22:36,021 --> 00:22:39,393
But on the bride's side, my father's name was gone.

435
00:22:39,843 --> 00:22:42,419
"Diane, my father's name is missing."

436
00:22:42,869 --> 00:22:43,532
"Oh, that.

437
00:22:43,532 --> 00:22:47,445
Well, there's only one of him, so it looked a little empty.

438
00:22:47,445 --> 00:22:51,026
The designer said it's cleaner to just use your name."

439
00:22:51,476 --> 00:22:52,682
My hands were shaking.

440
00:22:52,682 --> 00:22:56,577
I couldn't believe she could talk about my mother being gone like that.

441
00:22:57,027 --> 00:22:59,195
"Please put my father's name on it.

442
00:22:59,195 --> 00:23:01,550
That's the one thing I won't give up."

443
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,867
Diane sighed and said she would add it.

444
00:23:04,867 --> 00:23:05,970
In small print.

445
00:23:05,970 --> 00:23:08,470
Less than half the size of theirs.

446
00:23:08,920 --> 00:23:10,985
That evening, I showed Dad the proof.

447
00:23:10,985 --> 00:23:16,231
He put on his reading glasses and ran his finger over that tiny line of print for a long time.

448
00:23:16,681 --> 00:23:19,273
"There's my name, right next to my girl's.

449
00:23:19,273 --> 00:23:20,137
That's enough.

450
00:23:20,137 --> 00:23:21,310
That's all I need."

451
00:23:22,210 --> 00:23:27,207
After Diane left, my father sat at his workbench for a long time.

452
00:23:27,657 --> 00:23:28,363
"Gracie.

453
00:23:28,363 --> 00:23:30,128
I've been thinking."

454
00:23:30,578 --> 00:23:32,311
"Yes, Dad?"

455
00:23:32,761 --> 00:23:33,877
"This workshop.

456
00:23:33,877 --> 00:23:37,523
If I sell it, I can probably get thirty thousand.

457
00:23:37,523 --> 00:23:40,649
Add my savings, and we're close to fifty."

458
00:23:41,099 --> 00:23:41,485
"Dad!

459
00:23:41,485 --> 00:23:43,570
Then where would you work?"

460
00:23:44,020 --> 00:23:45,579
"I'm getting old anyway.

461
00:23:45,579 --> 00:23:47,269
Maybe it's time to retire.

462
00:23:47,269 --> 00:23:52,858
Seeing you walk into that family with your head held high, that's what matters to me."

463
00:23:53,308 --> 00:23:55,686
I burst into tears right there.

464
00:23:55,686 --> 00:23:59,521
In my first life, he really did sell the workshop.

465
00:23:59,521 --> 00:24:02,743
I didn't find out until after the wedding.

466
00:24:02,743 --> 00:24:07,882
Without his work, he grew weaker and weaker, in body and in spirit.

467
00:24:08,332 --> 00:24:09,765
"Dad, listen to me.

468
00:24:09,765 --> 00:24:12,329
You are not selling this workshop.

469
00:24:12,329 --> 00:24:13,837
Give me three weeks.

470
00:24:13,837 --> 00:24:15,119
Just three weeks.

471
00:24:15,119 --> 00:24:17,382
I'll take care of everything."

472
00:24:17,832 --> 00:24:20,461
"How are you going to take care of anything?"

473
00:24:20,911 --> 00:24:23,811
"Your daughter is smarter than you think.

474
00:24:23,811 --> 00:24:25,014
Do you trust me?"

475
00:24:25,464 --> 00:24:27,869
He looked at me for a long moment.

476
00:24:27,869 --> 00:24:29,566
Then, slowly, he nodded.

477
00:24:30,466 --> 00:24:35,492
A few days later, I noticed the drawer in Dad's bedroom was open.

478
00:24:35,492 --> 00:24:38,043
The drawer he always kept locked.

479
00:24:38,493 --> 00:24:40,644
The little velvet box was gone.

480
00:24:40,644 --> 00:24:43,280
The box with my mother's wedding ring.

481
00:24:43,730 --> 00:24:46,431
I ran through town like a crazy person.

482
00:24:46,431 --> 00:24:51,901
And I found him, at the end of Main Street, standing in front of the pawn shop.

483
00:24:51,901 --> 00:24:56,195
He was holding the box, staring at the glass door, not moving.

484
00:24:56,645 --> 00:24:57,695
"Dad!"

485
00:24:58,145 --> 00:25:00,417
He turned around, startled.

486
00:25:00,417 --> 00:25:01,933
His eyes were red.

487
00:25:02,383 --> 00:25:02,922
"Gracie.

488
00:25:02,922 --> 00:25:04,879
This will get a few thousand.

489
00:25:04,879 --> 00:25:06,634
Your mom would understand.

490
00:25:06,634 --> 00:25:09,063
It's for her little girl's wedding."

491
00:25:09,513 --> 00:25:12,782
"Dad, that's the only thing Mom left you.

492
00:25:12,782 --> 00:25:13,341
Please.

493
00:25:13,341 --> 00:25:14,457
Please don't."

494
00:25:14,907 --> 00:25:18,722
I threw my arms around him right there on the sidewalk.

495
00:25:18,722 --> 00:25:20,040
It started to rain.

496
00:25:20,040 --> 00:25:27,114
My father buried his face in my shoulder and, for the first time in my life, I heard him cry out loud.

497
00:25:27,564 --> 00:25:29,528
"I'm such a failure, Gracie.

498
00:25:29,528 --> 00:25:31,772
This is all I have to give you."

499
00:25:32,222 --> 00:25:34,001
That day, I decided.

500
00:25:34,001 --> 00:25:37,202
I would end this before the wedding.

501
00:25:37,202 --> 00:25:38,535
No matter what.

502
00:25:39,435 --> 00:25:43,801
Two weeks before the wedding, the day I remembered finally came.

503
00:25:44,251 --> 00:25:47,916
Diane asked me to meet her at an upscale café downtown.

504
00:25:47,916 --> 00:25:52,448
Sitting next to her was a man in a leather jacket with a gold watch.

505
00:25:52,448 --> 00:25:53,248
Gary Pruitt.

506
00:25:53,698 --> 00:25:54,930
"Grace, say hello.

507
00:25:54,930 --> 00:25:58,011
Gary helps our family with business matters."

508
00:25:58,461 --> 00:26:01,512
Gary slid a folder across the table.

509
00:26:01,962 --> 00:26:02,930
"Simple stuff.

510
00:26:02,930 --> 00:26:06,040
The family needs a little short-term capital.

511
00:26:06,040 --> 00:26:10,118
You sign, and in three months everything's back to normal."

512
00:26:10,568 --> 00:26:15,348
The document was titled "Commercial Mortgage and Security Agreement."

513
00:26:15,348 --> 00:26:17,980
Property: my building on Maple Street.

514
00:26:17,980 --> 00:26:21,513
Amount secured: two hundred forty thousand dollars.

515
00:26:21,963 --> 00:26:25,215
In my first life, I didn't read a single line.

516
00:26:25,215 --> 00:26:29,246
Diane said, "Family doesn't need to read the fine print,"

517
00:26:29,246 --> 00:26:30,589
and I believed her.

518
00:26:31,039 --> 00:26:33,326
This time, I read it slowly.

519
00:26:33,326 --> 00:26:34,388
Line by line.

520
00:26:34,388 --> 00:26:37,247
And my phone was already recording.

521
00:26:37,697 --> 00:26:42,852
"Diane, so this puts a mortgage on my building for two hundred forty thousand dollars?"

522
00:26:43,302 --> 00:26:44,729
"Oh, don't be dramatic.

523
00:26:44,729 --> 00:26:46,837
It's just a temporary arrangement.

524
00:26:46,837 --> 00:26:50,248
Are you saying you don't trust your own mother-in-law?"

525
00:26:50,698 --> 00:26:52,693
"Does Richard know about this?"

526
00:26:53,143 --> 00:26:54,772
Her eyes went sharp.

527
00:26:55,222 --> 00:26:56,699
"Do not tell Richard.

528
00:26:56,699 --> 00:27:00,077
If he finds out, it will tear this family apart.

529
00:27:00,077 --> 00:27:02,048
This stays between us women.

530
00:27:02,048 --> 00:27:02,822
Understand?

531
00:27:02,822 --> 00:27:06,904
And if you don't sign, you can forget about this wedding."

532
00:27:07,354 --> 00:27:08,878
I put the pen down.

533
00:27:09,328 --> 00:27:11,992
"Let me think about it for one day.

534
00:27:11,992 --> 00:27:13,286
It's my building.

535
00:27:13,286 --> 00:27:15,797
I have a few things to sort out."

536
00:27:16,247 --> 00:27:18,306
Diane clicked her tongue.

537
00:27:18,306 --> 00:27:19,047
"One day.

538
00:27:19,047 --> 00:27:20,612
Not a minute more."

539
00:27:21,512 --> 00:27:24,614
As I was leaving, Diane called after me.

540
00:27:25,064 --> 00:27:26,404
"Oh, one more thing.

541
00:27:26,404 --> 00:27:30,490
I've put your father at the very last table at the reception.

542
00:27:30,490 --> 00:27:34,040
The front tables are for Richard's business partners.

543
00:27:34,040 --> 00:27:35,112
You understand."

544
00:27:35,562 --> 00:27:36,665
I nodded.

545
00:27:37,115 --> 00:27:38,609
"And those wooden spoons.

546
00:27:38,609 --> 00:27:41,718
Don't even think about bringing them to the wedding.

547
00:27:41,718 --> 00:27:43,213
It would be humiliating."

548
00:27:43,663 --> 00:27:47,601
She climbed into Gary's black sedan, and they drove off.

549
00:27:47,601 --> 00:27:50,132
I stood on the curb for a long time.

550
00:27:50,582 --> 00:27:52,741
I now had eleven recordings.

551
00:27:52,741 --> 00:27:54,205
The county records.

552
00:27:54,205 --> 00:27:55,979
A photo of Gary's card.

553
00:27:55,979 --> 00:27:58,446
Pictures of the mortgage papers.

554
00:27:58,446 --> 00:28:01,684
Every piece of the puzzle was in my hands.

555
00:28:02,134 --> 00:28:04,122
There was only one thing left.

556
00:28:04,122 --> 00:28:08,763
To hand those pieces to the right person, at exactly the right moment.

557
00:28:09,663 --> 00:28:14,082
From the café, I drove straight to the cemetery where my mother is buried.

558
00:28:14,532 --> 00:28:15,928
The sun was going down.

559
00:28:15,928 --> 00:28:22,057
The little garden around her headstone was bright with marigolds my father had planted in the spring.

560
00:28:22,507 --> 00:28:24,040
"Mom, I'm so scared.

561
00:28:24,040 --> 00:28:27,182
In my first life, I couldn't protect Dad.

562
00:28:27,182 --> 00:28:28,868
What if I fail again?"

563
00:28:29,318 --> 00:28:31,712
Of course, there was no answer.

564
00:28:31,712 --> 00:28:35,418
But I remembered an old photo from my childhood.

565
00:28:35,418 --> 00:28:40,205
Me on my father's lap, his big rough hands covered in sawdust.

566
00:28:40,205 --> 00:28:45,842
With those hands, he had never done a single dishonest thing in his life.

567
00:28:46,292 --> 00:28:49,947
"Mom, I'm not going to just keep quiet this time.

568
00:28:49,947 --> 00:28:51,365
I'm going to fight.

569
00:28:51,365 --> 00:28:53,528
But I'm going to fight smart.

570
00:28:53,528 --> 00:28:56,289
This time, I'll protect Dad's hands."

571
00:28:56,739 --> 00:29:00,188
As I walked back to my car, my phone buzzed.

572
00:29:00,188 --> 00:29:01,206
It was Diane.

573
00:29:02,106 --> 00:29:03,999
It was a text message.

574
00:29:04,449 --> 00:29:06,971
"If I don't have your signature by ten a.m.

575
00:29:06,971 --> 00:29:11,135
tomorrow, your father will never set foot in this family's house again.

576
00:29:11,135 --> 00:29:13,130
Who does a carpenter think he is?"

577
00:29:13,580 --> 00:29:17,150
As I read it, something inside me went very quiet.

578
00:29:17,150 --> 00:29:18,578
And then it snapped.

579
00:29:18,578 --> 00:29:22,648
That message trampled on the last of my father's dignity.

580
00:29:22,648 --> 00:29:30,788
And while I sat in my car, Diane was posting pictures from a wine bar with her friends, a glass raised in a toast.

581
00:29:31,238 --> 00:29:32,674
I picked up my phone.

582
00:29:32,674 --> 00:29:38,077
And I dialed a number I had saved just once, when we were planning the wedding.

583
00:29:38,527 --> 00:29:40,156
Richard Whitmore.

584
00:29:40,606 --> 00:29:42,233
It rang three times.

585
00:29:42,233 --> 00:29:44,918
Then a deep, calm voice answered.

586
00:29:45,368 --> 00:29:46,010
"Grace?

587
00:29:46,010 --> 00:29:46,926
It's late.

588
00:29:46,926 --> 00:29:48,208
What's wrong?"

589
00:29:48,658 --> 00:29:51,352
"Richard, I'm so sorry to call at this hour.

590
00:29:51,352 --> 00:29:53,616
There's something I have to show you.

591
00:29:53,616 --> 00:29:55,392
I need to see you in person."

592
00:29:55,842 --> 00:29:57,488
There was a long pause.

593
00:29:57,488 --> 00:29:58,418
Then he said,

594
00:29:58,868 --> 00:30:00,710
"Seven tomorrow morning.

595
00:30:00,710 --> 00:30:03,627
The diner on Route Nine, by my office.

596
00:30:03,627 --> 00:30:05,392
And bring your father."

597
00:30:06,292 --> 00:30:10,331
At seven the next morning, my father and I walked into a little roadside diner.

598
00:30:10,331 --> 00:30:15,393
It was not the kind of place you'd expect to find a man who owned half the buildings in the county.

599
00:30:15,843 --> 00:30:18,988
Richard was already there, in a canvas work jacket.

600
00:30:18,988 --> 00:30:23,367
When he saw my father, he stood up and shook his hand with both of his.

601
00:30:23,817 --> 00:30:24,165
"Tom.

602
00:30:24,165 --> 00:30:27,502
At that dinner, I didn't even offer you a chair.

603
00:30:27,502 --> 00:30:29,032
I'm ashamed of myself.

604
00:30:29,032 --> 00:30:30,283
Please, sit down."

605
00:30:30,733 --> 00:30:33,363
My father didn't know what to say.

606
00:30:33,813 --> 00:30:36,150
I laid everything out on the table.

607
00:30:36,150 --> 00:30:38,553
The recording from the country club.

608
00:30:38,553 --> 00:30:40,222
The photo of Gary's card.

609
00:30:40,222 --> 00:30:42,091
The recording from the café.

610
00:30:42,091 --> 00:30:44,227
Pictures of the mortgage papers.

611
00:30:44,227 --> 00:30:46,230
And last night's text message.

612
00:30:46,680 --> 00:30:50,385
Richard listened to every recording, all the way through.

613
00:30:50,385 --> 00:30:54,416
When he took out the earbuds, he said nothing for a long time.

614
00:30:54,416 --> 00:30:55,782
His coffee went cold.

615
00:30:56,232 --> 00:30:58,333
"Two hundred thousand, you said."

616
00:30:58,783 --> 00:30:59,504
"Yes, sir.

617
00:30:59,504 --> 00:31:01,306
Summit Capital Partners."

618
00:31:01,756 --> 00:31:03,859
He let out a long breath.

619
00:31:04,309 --> 00:31:06,000
"I knew something was wrong.

620
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:09,684
She kept asking about the limits on the company credit cards.

621
00:31:09,684 --> 00:31:12,221
She was looking for the deed to the house.

622
00:31:12,221 --> 00:31:13,791
I pretended not to notice.

623
00:31:13,791 --> 00:31:14,999
That was my mistake.

624
00:31:14,999 --> 00:31:18,200
Grace, did you put all of this together by yourself?"

625
00:31:18,650 --> 00:31:19,911
"Yes, sir."

626
00:31:20,361 --> 00:31:22,566
Richard turned to my father.

627
00:31:23,016 --> 00:31:25,393
"Tom, you raised a remarkable daughter.

628
00:31:25,393 --> 00:31:29,172
If not for her, I would have found out after it was all gone."

629
00:31:29,622 --> 00:31:32,251
Then he took out his phone and made a call.

630
00:31:32,701 --> 00:31:33,053
"Jim.

631
00:31:33,053 --> 00:31:33,971
It's Richard.

632
00:31:33,971 --> 00:31:35,665
I need you this morning.

633
00:31:35,665 --> 00:31:39,194
And tonight, I want the whole family at the house.

634
00:31:39,194 --> 00:31:41,171
The Millers are coming too."

635
00:31:41,621 --> 00:31:44,670
Every piece of the puzzle was falling into place.

636
00:31:45,570 --> 00:31:49,177
In the parking lot, Richard took my father's hand again.

637
00:31:49,177 --> 00:31:51,559
He looked down at it for a long time.

638
00:31:51,559 --> 00:31:55,358
Calluses, little scars, sawdust ground deep into the lines.

639
00:31:55,808 --> 00:31:57,643
"Tom, those are good hands.

640
00:31:57,643 --> 00:31:58,594
Honest hands."

641
00:31:59,044 --> 00:32:02,069
My father, embarrassed, tried to pull away.

642
00:32:02,069 --> 00:32:03,618
Richard didn't let go.

643
00:32:04,068 --> 00:32:06,809
"Until tonight, act like nothing has happened.

644
00:32:06,809 --> 00:32:08,596
Not to Diane, not to the kids.

645
00:32:08,596 --> 00:32:09,907
I'll take care of it."

646
00:32:10,357 --> 00:32:11,987
Then he looked at me.

647
00:32:12,437 --> 00:32:12,883
"Grace.

648
00:32:12,883 --> 00:32:16,131
Tonight, pretend you're bringing the signed papers.

649
00:32:16,131 --> 00:32:20,908
If she thinks she's won, she'll say everything out loud, in her own words."

650
00:32:21,358 --> 00:32:22,028
I nodded.

651
00:32:22,028 --> 00:32:25,749
He had been married to her for almost forty years.

652
00:32:25,749 --> 00:32:27,460
Nobody knew her better.

653
00:32:28,360 --> 00:32:33,463
That evening, the whole family gathered in the Whitmores' big living room.

654
00:32:33,463 --> 00:32:36,566
Diane, Ethan, Brittany, and my father and me.

655
00:32:37,016 --> 00:32:38,847
Diane was in a wonderful mood.

656
00:32:38,847 --> 00:32:41,654
She thought I had come with the signed papers.

657
00:32:41,654 --> 00:32:45,804
She spread the same contribution list across the dining table again.

658
00:32:46,254 --> 00:32:48,259
"Tom, so glad you could make it.

659
00:32:48,259 --> 00:32:51,015
Looks like Grace finally came to her senses.

660
00:32:51,015 --> 00:32:54,084
Now, let's finish talking about the contribution.

661
00:32:54,084 --> 00:32:56,778
Grace, you brought the papers, didn't you?"

662
00:32:57,228 --> 00:33:00,358
Once again, there was no chair for my father.

663
00:33:00,358 --> 00:33:03,489
Diane had moved it out of the way on purpose.

664
00:33:03,939 --> 00:33:05,768
Then the study door opened.

665
00:33:05,768 --> 00:33:07,055
Richard walked out.

666
00:33:07,055 --> 00:33:11,526
Behind him came a man in a gray suit carrying a leather briefcase.

667
00:33:11,526 --> 00:33:13,830
Jim Lawson, the family's attorney.

668
00:33:14,280 --> 00:33:19,697
Without a word, Richard picked up a chair from the study and carried it over himself.

669
00:33:19,697 --> 00:33:22,119
He set it down right behind my father.

670
00:33:22,569 --> 00:33:23,957
"Tom, please sit down.

671
00:33:23,957 --> 00:33:27,300
Nobody in this house will ever make you stand again."

672
00:33:27,750 --> 00:33:28,981
Diane's face froze.

673
00:33:28,981 --> 00:33:31,701
Richard picked up the list from the table.

674
00:33:31,701 --> 00:33:35,587
He looked straight at his wife, and in a low voice, he said,

675
00:33:36,037 --> 00:33:37,443
"Put that away, Diane.

676
00:33:37,443 --> 00:33:40,192
Before you embarrass yourself any further."

677
00:33:41,092 --> 00:33:43,510
The room went completely silent.

678
00:33:43,960 --> 00:33:45,624
"Richard, what are you doing?

679
00:33:45,624 --> 00:33:49,010
This is all for Ethan's sake, for the family's reputation."

680
00:33:49,460 --> 00:33:50,460
"Reputation?

681
00:33:50,460 --> 00:33:52,459
Did you say reputation?"

682
00:33:52,909 --> 00:33:54,332
Richard nodded to Jim.

683
00:33:54,332 --> 00:33:57,438
One by one, Jim laid the documents on the table.

684
00:33:57,438 --> 00:34:00,608
A copy of the loan agreement from Summit Capital.

685
00:34:00,608 --> 00:34:01,902
The mortgage papers.

686
00:34:01,902 --> 00:34:04,167
And a transcript of the recordings.

687
00:34:04,617 --> 00:34:08,138
"Two hundred thousand dollars you borrowed behind my back.

688
00:34:08,138 --> 00:34:15,666
And to pay it off, you tried to put a lien on a building that belongs to a girl who isn't even married into this family yet.

689
00:34:15,666 --> 00:34:20,826
The fifty thousand you demanded from Tom, that was to plug the same hole, wasn't it?"

690
00:34:21,276 --> 00:34:23,955
The color drained from Diane's face.

691
00:34:24,405 --> 00:34:26,429
"It's, it's a misunderstanding.

692
00:34:26,429 --> 00:34:28,519
Richard, please, just this once.

693
00:34:28,519 --> 00:34:31,718
Gary said my money would double in three months."

694
00:34:32,168 --> 00:34:36,637
"Gary Pruitt had a fraud complaint filed against him this afternoon.

695
00:34:36,637 --> 00:34:38,411
You're not his only victim.

696
00:34:38,411 --> 00:34:39,923
There are seven others.

697
00:34:39,923 --> 00:34:41,566
You didn't invest, Diane.

698
00:34:41,566 --> 00:34:42,749
You were swindled.

699
00:34:42,749 --> 00:34:47,743
And you nearly handed that swindler another family's daughter's livelihood."

700
00:34:48,193 --> 00:34:50,623
Diane sank down onto the sofa.

701
00:34:50,623 --> 00:34:52,244
Her pearls trembled.

702
00:34:52,694 --> 00:34:55,141
"And the text you sent Grace last night.

703
00:34:55,141 --> 00:34:59,792
'Who does a carpenter think he is.' Do you know how many times I read that?"

704
00:35:00,242 --> 00:35:03,398
For the first time, Richard's voice shook.

705
00:35:03,848 --> 00:35:05,946
"My father was a carpenter, Diane.

706
00:35:05,946 --> 00:35:08,969
I started swinging a hammer when I was seventeen.

707
00:35:08,969 --> 00:35:12,672
This house, this company, all of it started with one hammer.

708
00:35:12,672 --> 00:35:14,214
I guess you forgot that."

709
00:35:15,114 --> 00:35:17,586
Jim took out one more sheet of paper.

710
00:35:18,036 --> 00:35:18,395
"Mrs.

711
00:35:18,395 --> 00:35:21,194
Whitmore, someone introduced you to Mr.

712
00:35:21,194 --> 00:35:21,697
Pruitt.

713
00:35:21,697 --> 00:35:23,491
Your friend, Linda Hayes.

714
00:35:23,491 --> 00:35:23,778
Mrs.

715
00:35:23,778 --> 00:35:28,084
Hayes received a ten thousand dollar referral fee from him."

716
00:35:28,534 --> 00:35:30,479
Diane's head snapped up.

717
00:35:30,929 --> 00:35:31,410
"Linda?

718
00:35:31,410 --> 00:35:32,647
That's impossible.

719
00:35:32,647 --> 00:35:35,190
We've been friends for thirty years."

720
00:35:35,640 --> 00:35:38,752
"She's one of the ladies you brought to my bakery,"

721
00:35:38,752 --> 00:35:39,729
I added quietly.

722
00:35:39,729 --> 00:35:41,742
"The one who talked the loudest."

723
00:35:42,192 --> 00:35:43,484
Diane went white.

724
00:35:43,484 --> 00:35:49,032
The friend she trusted most had seen her as prey from the very beginning.

725
00:35:49,482 --> 00:35:55,995
Brittany put down her phone and said in a small voice, "Mom, Aunt Linda pitched that investment to me too.

726
00:35:55,995 --> 00:35:59,006
I thought it was fine because you were in on it."

727
00:35:59,456 --> 00:36:02,784
The only sound in the room was Diane's ragged breathing.

728
00:36:02,784 --> 00:36:07,717
The mouth that had been so quick to tear other people down had nothing left to say.

729
00:36:08,617 --> 00:36:11,561
Right there, Richard made three decisions.

730
00:36:12,011 --> 00:36:16,617
First, there would be no contribution from the bride's family.

731
00:36:16,617 --> 00:36:19,515
No cash, no fur coat, no designer bags.

732
00:36:19,515 --> 00:36:20,109
Nothing.

733
00:36:20,559 --> 00:36:27,291
Second, the two hundred thousand dollar debt would be paid by selling Diane's lake house and her jewelry.

734
00:36:27,291 --> 00:36:29,920
Not one cent would come from the company.

735
00:36:29,920 --> 00:36:34,344
And from now on, Richard would manage the household accounts himself.

736
00:36:34,794 --> 00:36:38,072
Third, Diane would apologize to my father.

737
00:36:38,072 --> 00:36:38,853
Sincerely.

738
00:36:38,853 --> 00:36:39,633
Right now.

739
00:36:40,083 --> 00:36:42,545
Diane trembled for a long time.

740
00:36:42,545 --> 00:36:45,800
Then she slowly walked over to my father.

741
00:36:45,800 --> 00:36:48,182
And she got down on her knees.

742
00:36:48,632 --> 00:36:51,067
"Tom, I, I was wrong.

743
00:36:51,067 --> 00:36:53,155
I am truly sorry."

744
00:36:53,605 --> 00:36:57,023
My father, flustered, helped her back to her feet.

745
00:36:57,473 --> 00:36:58,902
"Please, ma'am, get up.

746
00:36:58,902 --> 00:36:59,461
I'm fine.

747
00:36:59,461 --> 00:37:01,945
All I want is for the kids to be happy."

748
00:37:02,845 --> 00:37:07,264
Then Ethan stepped forward, and he stood in front of his mother.

749
00:37:07,714 --> 00:37:10,469
"Mom, at that dinner, I didn't say a word.

750
00:37:10,469 --> 00:37:14,339
Not even when Tom was on the floor picking up those spoons.

751
00:37:14,339 --> 00:37:18,078
I'm going to be ashamed of that for the rest of my life."

752
00:37:18,528 --> 00:37:21,947
"Ethan, honey, I only wanted what's best for you."

753
00:37:22,397 --> 00:37:22,952
"No, Mom.

754
00:37:22,952 --> 00:37:25,296
You wanted what's best for your image.

755
00:37:25,296 --> 00:37:27,208
Grace never asked for anything.

756
00:37:27,208 --> 00:37:29,922
We were the ones trying to take her bakery."

757
00:37:30,372 --> 00:37:33,231
Diane looked at her son for a long moment.

758
00:37:33,231 --> 00:37:35,000
Then she lowered her eyes.

759
00:37:35,450 --> 00:37:38,342
Ethan turned to my father and bowed his head.

760
00:37:38,792 --> 00:37:42,127
"Tom, on the wedding day, please walk Grace down the aisle.

761
00:37:42,127 --> 00:37:42,862
In that suit.

762
00:37:42,862 --> 00:37:45,632
I think it's the best-looking suit in the world."

763
00:37:46,082 --> 00:37:48,055
My father couldn't speak.

764
00:37:48,055 --> 00:37:51,449
He just patted Ethan twice on the shoulder.

765
00:37:52,349 --> 00:37:55,400
The next morning, it was in the local news.

766
00:37:55,850 --> 00:38:00,320
"Investment advisor arrested in scheme targeting eight local women."

767
00:38:00,770 --> 00:38:04,778
The doors of Summit Capital Partners were sealed by federal agents.

768
00:38:04,778 --> 00:38:09,264
Gary Pruitt was arrested at the airport, trying to board a flight to Miami.

769
00:38:09,264 --> 00:38:11,716
Linda Hayes was charged as an accomplice.

770
00:38:12,166 --> 00:38:16,483
That afternoon, Diane tried to return her new fur coat to the department store.

771
00:38:16,483 --> 00:38:19,980
They told her it had already been worn and couldn't be returned.

772
00:38:19,980 --> 00:38:22,384
She stood outside the store for a long time.

773
00:38:22,384 --> 00:38:26,318
A week later, the coat showed up on an online marketplace at half price.

774
00:38:26,768 --> 00:38:30,667
At Diane's country club luncheon, everyone was whispering.

775
00:38:30,667 --> 00:38:36,045
She had tried to take a young woman's bakery and had disgraced her whole family.

776
00:38:36,045 --> 00:38:40,818
The reputation she had guarded so fiercely fell to the floor overnight.

777
00:38:41,268 --> 00:38:45,784
The woman who loved to cut other people down had finally cut herself.

778
00:38:46,684 --> 00:38:49,524
On my wedding day, the sky was perfectly clear.

779
00:38:49,524 --> 00:38:52,364
Then it was time for me to walk down the aisle.

780
00:38:52,814 --> 00:38:55,741
The doors opened, and I took my father's arm.

781
00:38:55,741 --> 00:38:57,303
He was wearing his suit.

782
00:38:57,303 --> 00:38:57,953
That suit.

783
00:38:57,953 --> 00:39:03,808
But on the cuffs there were new gold buttons, a gift Richard had quietly had made for him.

784
00:39:04,258 --> 00:39:04,986
One step.

785
00:39:04,986 --> 00:39:06,038
Then another.

786
00:39:06,038 --> 00:39:09,275
My father's hand was trembling a little.

787
00:39:09,275 --> 00:39:10,569
I held on tight.

788
00:39:11,019 --> 00:39:12,132
"Slow down, Dad.

789
00:39:12,132 --> 00:39:15,541
We've been waiting for this day our whole lives."

790
00:39:15,991 --> 00:39:16,705
He smiled.

791
00:39:16,705 --> 00:39:20,200
I will never forget that smile as long as I live.

792
00:39:20,650 --> 00:39:22,617
In the very front row sat Mrs.

793
00:39:22,617 --> 00:39:25,633
Patterson and our neighbors from Maple Street.

794
00:39:25,633 --> 00:39:27,797
Richard had invited them himself.

795
00:39:27,797 --> 00:39:29,043
Not the last table.

796
00:39:29,043 --> 00:39:29,961
The front row.

797
00:39:30,861 --> 00:39:36,238
At the reception, there was a small wooden box at every guest's place.

798
00:39:36,238 --> 00:39:40,463
Inside each box was a walnut spoon carved by my father.

799
00:39:40,463 --> 00:39:43,766
Richard had ordered them as wedding favors.

800
00:39:43,766 --> 00:39:45,302
Two hundred of them.

801
00:39:45,302 --> 00:39:47,069
And he paid full price.

802
00:39:47,519 --> 00:39:52,132
In his toast, Richard said, "Today I'm not only gaining a daughter-in-law.

803
00:39:52,132 --> 00:39:55,935
I'm gaining a friend I will respect for the rest of my life."

804
00:39:56,385 --> 00:40:01,812
Then he picked up one of my father's spoons and ate his soup with it.

805
00:40:01,812 --> 00:40:03,856
And he enjoyed every bite.

806
00:40:04,306 --> 00:40:07,563
Diane sat quietly among the guests, her eyes down.

807
00:40:07,563 --> 00:40:08,475
No lake house.

808
00:40:08,475 --> 00:40:09,256
No fur coat.

809
00:40:09,256 --> 00:40:12,253
Her friends from the club had stopped calling.

810
00:40:12,253 --> 00:40:16,356
The pain she had caused others had come right back home to her.

811
00:40:17,256 --> 00:40:20,464
Let me tell you what happened after that.

812
00:40:20,914 --> 00:40:24,190
My father's workshop is still standing at the end of our road.

813
00:40:24,190 --> 00:40:30,318
These days, he's building the chairs and tables for a new senior living center that Richard's company is putting up.

814
00:40:30,318 --> 00:40:32,326
He has taken on two young apprentices.

815
00:40:32,326 --> 00:40:33,858
And his cough is much better.

816
00:40:34,308 --> 00:40:37,668
My father's wooden trays are back in my display case.

817
00:40:37,668 --> 00:40:40,140
Diane comes to the bakery once a month.

818
00:40:40,140 --> 00:40:41,662
It was awkward at first.

819
00:40:41,662 --> 00:40:44,641
But now she buys two cinnamon rolls every time.

820
00:40:44,641 --> 00:40:46,353
People don't change easily.

821
00:40:46,353 --> 00:40:50,727
But a person who learns to feel ashamed can change, little by little.

822
00:40:51,177 --> 00:40:54,286
In my first life, all I knew how to do was endure.

823
00:40:54,286 --> 00:40:59,321
In my second life, I learned that enduring and protecting are not the same thing.

824
00:40:59,321 --> 00:41:03,300
If you want to protect the people you love, you have to prepare.

825
00:41:03,300 --> 00:41:04,543
Quietly, but firmly.

826
00:41:05,443 --> 00:41:10,965
A year after the wedding, Diane came into the bakery holding a small cloth bundle.

827
00:41:11,415 --> 00:41:11,947
"Grace.

828
00:41:11,947 --> 00:41:13,620
Do you remember this?"

829
00:41:14,070 --> 00:41:16,139
Inside was one walnut spoon.

830
00:41:16,139 --> 00:41:20,870
The very spoon that had fallen on the floor of the country club.

831
00:41:20,870 --> 00:41:26,117
My father had picked it up, and later he quietly mailed it back to her.

832
00:41:26,567 --> 00:41:29,752
"I've eaten with this spoon every day for a year.

833
00:41:29,752 --> 00:41:32,872
At first, I was so ashamed I hid it in a drawer.

834
00:41:32,872 --> 00:41:33,976
But it's strange.

835
00:41:33,976 --> 00:41:36,511
Food tastes warmer when I eat with it."

836
00:41:36,961 --> 00:41:39,905
Her eyes filled with tears.

837
00:41:40,355 --> 00:41:43,245
"Would you ask your father something for me?

838
00:41:43,245 --> 00:41:45,084
Could he carve one more set?

839
00:41:45,084 --> 00:41:48,105
I'd like to give it to our grandchild someday.

840
00:41:48,105 --> 00:41:49,878
And I'll pay him properly."

841
00:41:50,328 --> 00:41:51,590
I smiled.

842
00:41:52,040 --> 00:41:54,015
"Diane, Dad won't take your money.

843
00:41:54,015 --> 00:41:57,037
But he'll probably ask you to come over for dinner."

844
00:41:57,487 --> 00:42:04,300
That evening, both our families sat down together in my father's workshop and shared a big pot of chili.

845
00:42:04,300 --> 00:42:07,116
Every one of us, eating with wooden spoons.

846
00:42:08,016 --> 00:42:11,698
So, friends, what did you think of today's story?

847
00:42:12,148 --> 00:42:14,303
Love is not measured in dollars.

848
00:42:14,303 --> 00:42:18,143
It's measured in three months of evenings at a workbench.

849
00:42:18,143 --> 00:42:26,092
It's measured in a father ready to sell everything he had, just so his daughter could walk in with her head held high.

850
00:42:26,542 --> 00:42:30,508
People who look down on others always pay the price in the end.

851
00:42:30,508 --> 00:42:36,487
And people who live honestly and quietly will, someday, meet someone who sees their true worth.

852
00:42:36,937 --> 00:42:41,841
If you've lived through something like this, please share your story in the comments.

853
00:42:41,841 --> 00:42:45,303
Your story might be exactly what someone else needs to hear.

854
00:42:45,753 --> 00:42:49,487
If you enjoyed this, please subscribe and tap the like button.

855
00:42:49,487 --> 00:42:52,136
It means the world to us here at Life Story.

856
00:42:52,136 --> 00:42:55,749
Until next time, take care of yourselves, and of each other.
